#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083588 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98259c60-776f-5d69-b4e4-d293fe366d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080895} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd23e7f-a0b6-59fe-a380-b47116c9b174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080896} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad83be8-ec80-5b3f-9e53-07def80047c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080897} {:answer/id #uuid "7638e0c3-014d-55a8-90c1-4b5e439bf1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080898} {:answer/id #uuid "76dd9fc6-50df-5992-be03-4ea0c5c873fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bd23e7f-a0b6-59fe-a380-b47116c9b174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080896} :answer-field/id #uuid "1009e9ba-8a5a-5de7-b4ed-6b76962873fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x) = 3x^{3} + 5x^{2} - 15x - 25$ has a root in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$, find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$-\\frac{5}{3}$\n$3$\n$5$\n$-15$\n$-25$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$-5$\n$0$\n$25$\n\n \n\n$3$\n$0$\n$-15$\n$0$\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}]}
